    "teacherReflection": "Did learners treat the drag of the sack as real work even though the sack did not rise? Which groups used friction and heat spontaneously to explain the 400 J in the pulley system? Did anyone confuse work with power, for example by saying the runner does more work than the walker? Were spring balance readings steady enough for useful data, and how could the procedure be improved (for example, more trials or a smoother pull)? Which learners participated least and how can roles be rotated in Lesson 8? What evidence will you need to see in Lesson 8 so that learners can explain MA, VR and efficiency without losing the link to energy conservation?",
    "summaryTablePrompt": {
      "observed": "We observed that dragging a sack of maize across rough ground needs a larger friction force (150 N) than on smooth concrete (100 N), so more work is done over the same 10 m (1500 J versus 1000 J). We also saw that the same stair climb (1500 J) takes 6 s when walking and 3 s when running, and that the pulley system needs 2000 J input in 20 s (100 W) while hand lifting needs 1600 J in 20 s (80 W).",
      "learned": "We learned that friction does negative work on a moving object, so a person must do work equal to friction x distance to keep it moving at steady speed, and that this energy is transferred as heat. We learned that power is the rate of doing work, P = W/t, measured in watts where 1 W = 1 J/s, and that doing the same work faster needs more power.",
      "explained": "The pulley system needs 2000 J of input although the bucket gains only 1600 J of PE because friction in the pulleys and rope transfers 400 J to heat, just as friction turns the work of dragging the maize sack into heat. No energy is destroyed. The pulley lets Mwende use a smaller 125 N force over a longer 16 m rope pull, and its useful output power of 80 W equals the hand lift power while its input power is 100 W."
    }
